Blueprint update stress measurements
====================================

This disposable mudlib is outside t-* and is run explicitly. Build the driver
with DEBUG, BLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING, and blueprint updates enabled. The Python
handle scenarios additionally require the optional Python package. For example,
from src, with the Python development package installed:

    ./configure --enable-debug --enable-blueprint-update --enable-swap-efun \
        --enable-use-python EXTRA_CFLAGS=-DBLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING
    make clean
    make

Other packages can be enabled as needed. Omit --enable-use-python and the Python
handle cases when building without that package. Then, from test:

    python3 blueprint_stress.py --driver ../src/ldmud \
        --cases blueprint_stress/cases.json --output /tmp/blueprint-stress-run

The evidence-join counterexamples can be checked without running a driver:

    python3 -m unittest discover -s blueprint_stress -p test_measurements.py

The output directory must not exist. Each bounded scenario gets fresh source,
logs, an inherited loopback listener, a process deadline, and the normal alarm
interval of two seconds. A nonzero exit or missing positive assertion/metric
marker fails the run. Configuration limits may require changing the expected
outcome in the case file; retain the original failed run as evidence. Use
--allow-unmeasured only for semantic control runs, never timing conclusions.

The JSON cases choose clone count, retained variable width, handle mode (0 none,
1 native function/variable closures, 2 Python function/variable wrappers), and
update cycles. late_target creates an additional clone after admission to test
execution-time cohort limits. batch adds independent source-only requests to
the same tick. churn acquires and releases fresh declaration handles after each
successful update. Every retained variable, clone identity and held handle is
checked across publication or rejection; successful later generations reorder
variables and add/remove a declaration. A failed request must leave every
cohort member on its preceding program and values.

The native selector blueprint-measure is read only in DEBUG/testing builds.
The batch timer brackets program_update_process(), including initial/final
report expiry. Each request timer includes selection, candidate compilation,
schema/default/report work, variable and binding preparation, publication, and
retirement. Setup, queue wait, polling and LPC verification are outside these
timers. The probe stores at most 64 requests in static scalar records and prints
them after both timers stop; overflowing this observation capacity invalidates
the measurement without changing request processing. The final nonallocating
validation and atomic commit contain no new observation calls.

Timing uses the driver's existing gettimeofday platform API. Negative or failed
clock readings invalidate the run. The launcher also compares elapsed wall and
monotonic clocks to reject discontinuities; this is not a monotonic native
timer. Request and batch microseconds include the small cost of scalar timing
and allocator-counter reads. They do not include the final metric output IO.

Allocator snapshots are taken at request entry, after the candidate and final
cohort are available, after all fallible preparation before final validation,
and after retirement/input release. The points bitmask records which snapshots
exist for an early failure. *_used reports xalloc_used() driver data bytes;
*_allocated reports xalloc_allocated(), including allocator overhead. These are
simultaneous-storage snapshots, not the compiler's transient high-water mark or
whole-process RSS. candidate_bytes is the program's total_size; it does not
include every temporary compiler allocation. System-allocator builds may lack
useful allocation accounting and should not supply these memory conclusions.

The Python verification also obtains a transient version-function wrapper; its
inactive binding key can remain in addition to the two held wrappers. The
Python cases therefore do not imply exactly two registry keys per clone.

Initial handle memory compares the same preallocated clone/array cohort before
and after creating handles. The native delta includes closures, returned handle
arrays, and binding headers/keys. Python runs report driver allocation separately
from shallow wrapper sizes and Python tracemalloc deltas; the latter include
Python cohort wrappers, tuples and stored hashes and are not a whole-process
memory measurement. The trace is stopped before update timing begins.

Each churn sample compares memory immediately before acquiring new declaration
handles, while held, and after releasing them within one LPC evaluation. Its
retained_delta therefore excludes reports accumulated between requests. Inactive
binding keys remain until object disposal, without retaining old programs; do
not interpret this intentional registry growth as a retained-program leak.

The request limits combine clone count, variable slots, bytes and work. A wide
layout or many Python handles can exhaust work before reaching the configured
clone/slot limit. Record observed successful and first rejected scenarios and
their exact configuration before proposing an operational clone count. These
ordinary-object fixtures provide no interactive/player-session acceptance.
